WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.420
Protobuf contracts define the service methods, message fields, field types, and streaming shape that clients and services agree to use.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.600
That contract is not just a technical artifact for code generation.

3
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:19.900
It is a security-relevant interface because it decides what data can be requested, submitted, returned, or streamed.

4
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.780
Messages should be narrow and purpose-driven.

5
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.960
A request message should contain the fields needed for the specific operation, not a broad copy of an internal domain object.

6
00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:35.660
A response message should return what the caller needs, not every field the service happens to know.

7
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.840
This reduces accidental exposure and makes the contract easier to review.

8
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:55.640
Contract review should look for internal identifiers, administrative fields, sensitive flags, secret values, overly broad status fields, unnecessary record details, and fields that might be confused with server-controlled decisions.

9
00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:05.740
If a field affects ownership, tenant, role, approval, pricing, configuration, or workflow state, reviewers should understand why the client may send or receive it.

10
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:11.780
Field additions, deprecated fields, and backward compatibility are security review topics.

11
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.120
A field that is no longer displayed may still be accepted by older clients.

12
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:22.620
A newly added field may appear in generated clients, documentation, logs, or downstream services.

13
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.640
Compatibility work should preserve security intent, not only serialization compatibility.

14
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:37.320
The choice between unary, server-streaming, client-streaming, and bidirectional-streaming methods should match business need and resource impact.

15
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:46.060
Streaming can be useful, but it changes how long calls last, how much data can flow, how cancellation works, and how operators monitor behavior.

16
00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:51.540
Code-first gRPC can be appropriate in all-.NET systems, but the same discipline applies.

17
00:01:51.640 --> 00:02:04.280
Whether the contract begins as a .proto file or C# types, teams still need data minimization, reviewable method shape, clear ownership, and careful compatibility decisions.
